   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let g(x) = x e^(x + 1). The line tangent to the graph of g at the point where x = -1 is y = L(x). Which of the following is L(x)?"
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": -1
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "By the product rule, g'(x) = e^(x + 1) + x e^(x + 1) = (x + 1) e^(x + 1).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Multiply",
            [
               "Add",
               "x",
               1
            ],
            [
               "Exp",
               [
                  "Add",
                  "x",
                  1
               ]
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The slope is g'(-1) = 0 times e^0 = 0.",
         "mathjson": 0
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "The height is g(-1) = (-1) e^0 = -1, so the point of tangency is (-1, -1).",
         "mathjson": -1
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "Point slope form gives y = -1 + 0(x + 1), which is the horizontal line y = -1.",
         "mathjson": -1
      }
   ],
